  <!-- Executive Summary -->
  <section>
    <h2>Executive Summary</h2>
    <p>This report analyzes 1,000 student records from eight districts in Bangladesh to identify the key drivers of academic success at the HSC level. The dataset spans demographic, socio-economic, behavioral, and institutional dimensions.</p>
    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Key Finding:</strong> The national average HSC GPA is <strong>{charts['kpis']['avg_hsc']}</strong>, with <strong>{charts['kpis']['excellent_rate']}%</strong> of students achieving excellent performance (≥4.5). However, a significant geographic disparity exists: Barisal leads with an average HSC GPA of 4.11, while Rangpur trails at 3.91—a gap of <strong>0.20 points</strong>. Notably, the overall GPA progression delta is slightly positive (+0.018), suggesting marginal system-wide improvement from previous GPA to HSC, but nearly half of all students (48.4%) experienced a decline.
    </div>
    <p>Surprisingly, conventional predictors show weak effects in this dataset: family income correlates only −0.007 with HSC results, attendance correlates −0.014, and study hours correlate −0.022. The most meaningful differences emerge at the district and school-type levels, where institutional context appears to outweigh individual behavioral inputs.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 1: Demographics -->
  <section id="demographic-overview">
    <h2>1. Student Demographic Overview</h2>
    <p>The sample comprises 1,000 students distributed across gender, age, district, and school type with reasonable balance, providing a representative snapshot of Bangladesh's HSC cohort.</p>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="chartGender"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="chartAge"></canvas></div>
