      <tr><td>Gender</td><td>Male</td><td>490</td><td>49.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Age</td><td>15</td><td>267</td><td>26.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Age</td><td>16</td><td>263</td><td>26.3%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Age</td><td>17</td><td>259</td><td>25.9%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Age</td><td>18</td><td>211</td><td>21.1%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>School Type</td><td>Private</td><td>524</td><td>52.4%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>School Type</td><td>Public</td><td>476</td><td>47.6%</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>District representation ranges from 117 students in Sylhet to 133 in Chattogram, ensuring no single region dominates the analysis. The near-even gender split (51% female, 49% male) and balanced school-type enrollment allow for meaningful comparative analysis across these dimensions.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 2: Academic Performance -->
  <section id="academic-performance-analysis">
    <h2>2. Academic Performance Analysis</h2>
    <p>Academic outcomes are measured across three time points—Previous GPA, SSC Result, and HSC Result—enabling longitudinal assessment of student progression through Bangladesh's 0–5 GPA scale.</p>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-container wide"><canvas id="chartLongitudinal"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-container wide"><canvas id="chartHSCClass"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <h3>Performance Distribution</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th>Previous GPA</th><th>SSC Result</th><th>HSC Result</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Mean</td><td>3.995</td><td>4.257</td><td>4.013</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Std Dev</td><td>0.585</td><td>0.429</td><td>0.589</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Min</td><td>3.00</td><td>3.50</td><td>3.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Max</td><td>5.00</td><td>5.00</td><td>5.00</td></tr>
    </table>
    <div class="warning-box">
      <strong>Progression Alert:</strong> While the mean SSC Result (4.26) exceeds both Previous GPA (3.99) and HSC Result (4.01), the HSC stage shows a <strong>drop of 0.24 GPA points</strong> from SSC. This "HSC dip" suggests that the transition to higher secondary education may present systemic challenges not fully captured by prior performance.
    </div>
    <p>HSC performance classification reveals that <strong>26.8%</strong> of students achieve Excellent (≥4.5), <strong>23.8%</strong> Good (4.0–4.49), <strong>49.4%</strong> Average (3.0–3.99), and <strong>0.0%</strong> Poor (&lt;3.0). The pass rate is 100%, but the absence of any student below 3.0 indicates either a floor effect in the dataset or strong baseline preparation. The more policy-relevant threshold is GPA &lt;3.5, which captures <strong>24.3%</strong> of students who may benefit from targeted intervention.</p>
    <h3>GPA Progression Delta</h3>
    <p>The average delta (HSC − Previous GPA) is <strong>+0.018</strong>, masking substantial heterogeneity: 50.7% of students improved, 48.4% declined, and 0.9% remained stable. The standard deviation of delta is 0.811, indicating wide variation in individual trajectories. Public school students show a notably higher delta (+0.042) than private school students (−0.005), and students with internet access improve more (+0.059) than those without (−0.020).</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 3: Socio-Economic Impact -->
  <section id="socioeconomic-impact">
    <h2>3. Socio-Economic Factors and Their Impact on Performance</h2>
    <p>This section examines how family income, parent education, internet access, and private tuition relate to HSC outcomes. <strong>Effect sizes and mean differences are reported for all comparisons.</strong></p>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-container wide"><canvas id="chartParentEd"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-container wide"><canvas id="chartIncome"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="chartInternet"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="chartTuition"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <h3>Parent Education</h3>
    <p>Parent education shows a non-linear relationship with student HSC performance. Students whose parents completed Secondary education achieve the highest mean HSC GPA (<strong>4.050</strong>), followed by Higher Secondary (<strong>4.035</strong>), Graduate (<strong>4.001</strong>), and Primary (<strong>3.964</strong>). The effect size between Primary and Secondary is small (Cohen's d = 0.146), and differences between Secondary, Higher Secondary, and Graduate are negligible. Statistical tests confirm no significant differences (all p &gt; 0.10).</p>
    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Policy Insight:</strong> The absence of a clear gradient suggests that parent education alone is not a strong lever for HSC improvement in this cohort. Interventions should not exclusively target students with less-educated parents, as the performance overlap across groups is substantial.
    </div>
    <h3>Family Income</h3>
    <p>Family income, measured in monthly BDT, shows virtually no correlation with HSC results (r = <strong>−0.007</strong>). Mean HSC GPA across income quartiles is remarkably flat:</p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Quartile</th><th>Income Range (BDT)</th><th>Mean HSC GPA</th><th>Delta vs Q1</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Q1 (Lowest)</td><td>10,016 – 22,748</td><td>4.023</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Q2</td><td>22,755 – 34,846</td><td>3.992</td><td>−0.031</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Q3</td><td>34,849 – 47,782</td><td>4.025</td><td>+0.002</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Q4 (Highest)</td><td>47,997 – 59,767</td><td>4.012</td><td>−0.011</td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>Notably, the highest-income quartile does not outperform the lowest. This finding challenges the assumption that income-based resource allocation will directly translate to academic gains at the HSC level.</p>
    <h3>Internet Access & Private Tuition</h3>
    <p>Internet access shows a marginal positive association: students with access average <strong>4.022</strong> versus <strong>4.004</strong> without (difference = +0.017, Cohen's d = 0.029, p = 0.647). Private tuition shows an even smaller effect: <strong>4.019</strong> with tuition versus <strong>4.007</strong> without (difference = +0.012, Cohen's d = 0.020, p = 0.746). Neither difference is statistically significant.</p>
    <div class="warning-box">
      <strong>Caution:</strong> While internet access and private tuition are often assumed to be strong predictors, this dataset shows no meaningful effect. This may reflect universal saturation (both are near 50% prevalence), measurement limitations, or the possibility that quality of use matters more than binary access.
    </div>
    <h3>Combined Socio-Economic Effects</h3>
    <p>Cross-tabulating internet access and private tuition reveals no interaction: the highest-performing subgroup is "Internet + Tuition" (4.023), but the lowest is "No Internet + No Tuition" (3.993)—a gap of only 0.030 points. Similarly, parent education combined with internet access shows no consistent pattern. The data do not support a simple "resource accumulation" model of academic success.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 4: Study Behavior & Attendance -->
  <section id="study-behavior-attendance">
    <h2>4. Study Behavior and Attendance Patterns</h2>
    <p>This section investigates the relationship between study hours, attendance, and HSC outcomes.</p>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-container wide"><canvas id="chartStudy"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-container wide"><canvas id="chartAttendance"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <h3>Study Hours per Week</h3>
    <p>The correlation between weekly study hours and HSC GPA is <strong>−0.022</strong>, indicating no meaningful linear relationship. When binned into Low (≤10h), Medium (11–20h), and High (&gt;20h), mean HSC GPAs are 4.034, 3.986, and 4.025 respectively. The medium group underperforms slightly, but differences are negligible.</p>
    <div class="insight-box">
      <strong>Interpretation:</strong> More study hours do not guarantee better outcomes. This may reflect differences in study quality, school-level teaching effectiveness, or reverse causation (weaker students studying more to compensate). Policy should focus on <em>structured study support</em> rather than simply increasing hours.
    </div>
    <h3>Attendance</h3>
    <p>Attendance correlates <strong>−0.014</strong> with HSC results—a counter-intuitive weak negative association. Mean HSC GPA by attendance tier: Low (≤70%) = 4.019, Medium (71–85%) = <strong>4.036</strong>, High (&gt;85%) = 3.982. The highest-attendance group actually scores lowest on average.</p>
    <p>One plausible explanation is that students with weaker academic foundations attend more to compensate, while higher-performing students may miss classes for private coaching or self-study. Alternatively, school quality may vary: students in less effective schools attend regularly but learn less per hour. Regardless, <strong>attendance alone is not a reliable predictor of HSC success</strong> in this dataset.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Section 5: Regional Disparities -->
  <section id="regional-disparities">
    <h2>5. Regional Performance Disparities</h2>
    <p>Geographic and institutional inequalities are among the most pronounced findings in this analysis. District-level mean HSC GPA spans a 0.20-point range, and public-private gaps vary dramatically by region.</p>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-container wide"><canvas id="chartDistrictHSC"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-container wide"><canvas id="chartDistrictType"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <div class="chart-grid">
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="chartExcellentDist"></canvas></div>
      <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="chartBelow35Dist"></canvas></div>
    </div>
    <h3>District Rankings</h3>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Rank</th><th>District</th><th>Mean HSC</th><th>Excellent Rate</th><th>Below 3.5 Rate</th><th>Progression Delta</th><th>n</th></tr>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Barisal</td><td>4.113</td><td>36.8%</td><td>21.6%</td><td>+0.040</td><td>125</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Rajshahi</td><td>4.091</td><td>32.8%</td><td>22.1%</td><td>+0.078</td><td>122</td></tr>
